CONTEXT (from the previous page). Gauss is finding the principal axes of a ternary quadratic form, i.e. the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the symmetric matrix M = [[A,c,b],[c,B,a],[b,a,C]]. Here a couples the 2nd and 3rd axes, b the 1st and 3rd, and c the 1st and 2nd. This assignment is my inference from his cofactors. For a root G of the cubic f(G) = det(G·I − M) = (G−A)(G−B)(G−C) − aa(G−A) − bb(G−B) − cc(G−C) − 2abc he sets
  𝔄 = a(G−A) + bc, 𝔅 = b(G−B) + ac, ℭ = c(G−C) + ab.
The direction cosines of the axis belonging to G are then proportional to (1/𝔄, 1/𝔅, 1/ℭ):
  α = (1/𝔄)/sqrt(1/𝔄² + 1/𝔅² + 1/ℭ²), etc.
He also derives α² = [(G−B)(G−C) − aa]/((G−G')(G−G'')), where Δ = f'(G) = 3GG − 2(A+B+C)G + AB+AC+BC − aa − bb − cc = (G−G')(G−G'').

